findings.body:
The HEAD commit changed `@Roles("TEAM_ADMIN")` to `@Roles("TEAM_MEMBER")`. In `RolesGuard`, `hasMinimumRole` returns `true` whenever the caller's `TEAM_<role>` index is less than or equal to the required index. `TEAM_MEMBER` is the last entry in `TEAM_ROLES`, so every membership passes. A plain member can therefore mint 7-day invite tokens, which is the authorization bypass the spec's "team members receive 403" requirement is meant to prevent. The e2e test at line 148 ("should fail to create invite as non-admin member") expects 403 and will get 200.

::diff{file="apps/api/v2/src/modules/teams/invite/controllers/teams-invite.controller.ts" lines="30-34"}

::callout{type="security"}
A member with no admin rights can create invite links for their own team. Restore the admin requirement.

::suggestion{file="apps/api/v2/src/modules/teams/invite/controllers/teams-invite.controller.ts" lines="32-32"}
  @Roles("TEAM_ADMIN")

In `createInvite`, the new code returns `token: newToken.identifier`. `identifier` is the constant string `invite-link-for-teamId-<teamId>`, so every new invite returns the same value. The random token is only used in `inviteLink`. The API therefore hands clients a token that doesn't match the link, and it's unusable for anything that needs the token. The e2e test "should create a new invite on each request" checks `token !== firstToken` (line 88), and that fails because both values are the identifier. The `toContain(token)` checks (lines 75 and 90) also fail.

::diff{file="packages/features/ee/teams/services/teamService.ts" lines="88-101"}

::callout{type="warning"}
This is shared service code. The tRPC `teams.createInvite` handler returns the same object, so the web-side result changes too. The existing-token path (lines 73-84) still returns `existingToken.token`, so the two paths now disagree.

::suggestion{file="packages/features/ee/teams/services/teamService.ts" lines="98-101"}
    return {
      token,
      inviteLink: await TeamService.buildInviteLink(token, isOrganizationOrATeamInOrganization),
    };

The original code was `if (!isOrgContext) return teamInviteLink;`, which sends regular teams to `/teams?token=` and org or org-sub-teams to `/signup?...&callbackUrl=<getting-started>`. The PR changes it to `if (isOrgContext)`, which flips both cases. The PR description and the endpoint's documented `inviteLink` example both describe the original routing. This also changes the web invite modal's output, since `buildInviteLink` is shared with the tRPC `createInvite` path, including the existing-token path. The checklist item "Confirm `TeamService.createInvite` generates correct links for both regular teams and org sub-teams" would fail on this line.

::diff{file="packages/features/ee/teams/services/teamService.ts" lines="104-111"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The fix is to restore the original negation. Org contexts should return the `/teams` link and regular teams should return the `/signup` link with the getting-started callback. Please double-check which context name the PR intends. The fix below reverts to the original code's meaning.

::suggestion{file="packages/features/ee/teams/services/teamService.ts" lines="106-106"}
    if (!isOrgContext) {

The e2e spec only checks that `inviteLink` is a string and that it contains the token. Nothing checks the path or the callback. That's how the inverted condition in `buildInviteLink` got through: a test that asserted `/teams` for a regular team and `/signup` with `callbackUrl` for an org sub-team would have caught it. The spec's own checklist asks for this verification and the spec doesn't provide it.

::diff{file="apps/api/v2/src/modules/teams/invite/controllers/teams-invite.controller.e2e-spec.ts" lines="67-77"}

Add a case for a regular team that asserts `inviteLink` starts with `/teams?token=`, and one for an org sub-team (or `isOrganization: true` team) that asserts `/signup?token=` and `callbackUrl=`. Keep the assertions on the exact prefix, not on `toContain`, so the routing is actually checked.

This PR changed the controller's `TeamService` import from `@calcom/platform-libraries` (the same entry the existing org invite controller and team membership services use) to a deep import into `@calcom/features/ee/teams/services/teamService`. This is the only file under `apps/api/v2/src` that imports from `@calcom/features/`. It will drift from the packaged library that the API runs against, and it might not resolve in the packaged build. The DTO is also duplicated. `apps/api/v2/src/modules/organizations/teams/invite/outputs/invite.output.ts` already defines `CreateInviteOutputDto`, and this PR adds a second copy under `teams/invite/outputs/`.

::diff{file="apps/api/v2/src/modules/teams/invite/controllers/teams-invite.controller.ts" lines="19-22"}

Switch the import back to `@calcom/platform-libraries`, matching `organizations-teams-invite.controller.ts`. Import `CreateInviteOutputDto` from the existing organizations DTO file or move it to a shared location, rather than keeping a second copy.

Each POST inserts a fresh `verificationToken` row valid for 7 days, and nothing limits how many a team can accumulate. Neither this controller nor the sibling `teams/memberships` controller applies a throttle, so this is a broader gap rather than something this PR introduced. Still, a repeated call from an admin key can grow the table without bound. This is worth a follow-up ticket, not a merge blocker.

::diff{file="apps/api/v2/src/modules/teams/invite/controllers/teams-invite.controller.ts" lines="30-38"}

Consider reusing a still-valid token for the team (the service already supports this through `options.token`) or applying a throttle on this route.
